############################################################
# Randomize so each student gets at least one from each group
############################################################

@structures = (
  random_subset(1, @h_bond),
  random_subset(2, @dipole_dipole),
  random_subset(2, @ldf),
);

@structures = random_subset(5, @structures);
############################################################
# Dropdown options
############################################################

@polarityOptions = (
  "polar",
  "nonpolar",
);

@IMFoptions = (
  "hydrogen bonding",
  "dipole-dipole forces",
  "London dispersion forces",
);

@polarityMenus = map {
  PopUp(
    ["", @polarityOptions],
    $_->{polarity}
  )
} @structures;

@IMFmenus = map {
  PopUp(
    ["", @IMFoptions],
    $_->{IMF}
  )
} @structures;
############################################################
# Problem text
############################################################

BEGIN_PGML

## Polarity and Intermolecular Forces

For each Lewis structure, categorize the molecule as polar or nonpolar. Then specifiy the strongest intermolecular force that would occur between molecules in a pure sample of this compound.

[#
[.Molecule.]
[.Lewis structure.]
[.Polar or nonpolar?.]
[.Strongest intermolecular force.]*{ headerrow => 1 }

[.[``\mathrm{[$structures[0]{formula}]}``].]
[.[@ image( "$structures[0]->{image}", width=>200, 
 extra_html_tags=>"alt='$structures[0]->{alt}'"  )@]*.]
[.[_]{$polarityMenus[0]}.]
[.[_]{$IMFmenus[0]}.]*

[.[``\mathrm{[$structures[1]{formula}]}``].]
[.[@ image( "$structures[1]->{image}", width=>200, 
 extra_html_tags=>"alt='$structures[1]->{alt}'"  )@]*.]
[.[_]{$polarityMenus[1]}.]
[.[_]{$IMFmenus[1]}.]*

[.[``\mathrm{[$structures[2]{formula}]}``].]
[.[@ image( "$structures[2]->{image}", width=>200, 
 extra_html_tags=>"alt='$structures[2]->{alt}'"  )@]*.]
[.[_]{$polarityMenus[2]}.]
[.[_]{$IMFmenus[2]}.]*

[.[``\mathrm{[$structures[3]{formula}]}``].]
[.[@ image( "$structures[3]->{image}", width=>200, 
 extra_html_tags=>"alt='$structures[3]->{alt}'"  )@]*.]
[.[_]{$polarityMenus[3]}.]
[.[_]{$IMFmenus[3]}.]*

[.[``\mathrm{[$structures[4]{formula}]}``].]
[.[@ image( "$structures[4]->{image}", width=>200, 
 extra_html_tags=>"alt='$structures[4]->{alt}'"  )@]*.]
[.[_]{$polarityMenus[4]}.]
[.[_]{$IMFmenus[4]}.]*

#]{align => '|c|c|c|c|', horizontalrules => 1}

END_PGML

############################################################
# Answer checkers
############################################################

for my $i (0..4) {
  ANS($polarityMenus[$i]->cmp());
  ANS($IMFmenus[$i]->cmp());
}

############################################################
# Hint
############################################################

BEGIN_PGML_HINT

First decide whether the molecule is polar or nonpolar.

A molecule is usually *nonpolar* if either:

* all of the bonds are nonpolar, or
* the molecule has polar bonds arranged symmetrically so the bond dipoles cancel.

A molecule is *polar* if it has polar bonds and the bond dipoles do not cancel.

After deciding whether the molecule is polar or nonpolar, choose the strongest intermolecular force.

Use these rules:

1. If the molecule has H directly bonded to N, O, or F, its strongest intermolecular force is hydrogen bonding.

2. If the molecule is polar but does not have H directly bonded to N, O, or F, its strongest intermolecular force is dipole-dipole forces.

3. If the molecule is nonpolar, its strongest intermolecular force is London dispersion forces.

Remember that all molecules have London dispersion forces, but this question asks for the strongest intermolecular force.

END_PGML_HINT

############################################################
# Solution
############################################################

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

The correct classifications are:

[#
[.Molecule.]
[.Polar or nonpolar?.]
[.Strongest intermolecular force.]*{ headerrow => 1 }

[.[``\mathrm{[$structures[0]{formula}]}``].]
[.[$structures[0]{polarity}].]
[.[$structures[0]{IMF}].]*

[.[``\mathrm{[$structures[1]{formula}]}``].]
[.[$structures[1]{polarity}].]
[.[$structures[1]{IMF}].]*

[.[``\mathrm{[$structures[2]{formula}]}``].]
[.[$structures[2]{polarity}].]
[.[$structures[2]{IMF}].]*

[.[``\mathrm{[$structures[3]{formula}]}``].]
[.[$structures[3]{polarity}].]
[.[$structures[3]{IMF}].]*

[.[``\mathrm{[$structures[4]{formula}]}``].]
[.[$structures[4]{polarity}].]
[.[$structures[4]{IMF}].]*

#]{align => '|c|c|c|', horizontalrules => 1}

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();